<article class="env-warning" id="00HH">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00HH">Warning <span data-tag="00HH">1.3.6.7</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a simplicial set. The proof of Proposition <a data-tag="004M" href="/tag/004M">1.3.6.4</a> gives a construction of the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{S}$ by generators and relations. The result of this construction is not always easy to describe. If $x$ and $y$ are vertices of $S$, then every morphism from $x$ to $y$ in $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{S}$ can be represented by a composition </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  [e_ n] \circ [e_{n-1}] \circ \cdots \circ [e_1], \]
</div>
<p> where $\{  e_ i \} _{0 \leq i \leq n}$ is a sequence of edges satisfying </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  d^{1}_1(e_1) = x \quad \quad d^{1}_0(e_ i) = d^{1}_1(e_{i+1}) \quad \quad d^{1}_0( e_ n ) = y. \]
</div>
<p> In general, it can be difficult to determine whether or not two such compositions represent the same morphism of $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{S}$ (even for finite simplicial sets, this question is algorithmically undecidable). However, there are two situations in which the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{S}$ admits a simpler description: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
Let $S$ be a simplicial set of dimension $\leq 1$, which we can identify with a directed graph $G$ (Proposition <a data-tag="001N" href="/tag/001N">1.1.6.9</a>). In this case, the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{S}$ is generated <em>freely</em> by the vertices and edges of the graph $G$: that is, it can be identified with the <em>path category</em> of $G$ (Proposition <a data-tag="00HP" href="/tag/00HP">1.3.7.5</a>) which we study in §<a data-tag="00HJ" href="/tag/00HJ">1.3.7</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Let $S$ be an $\infty $-category. In this case, every morphism in the homotopy category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{S}$ can be represented by a single edge of $S$, rather than a composition of edges (in other words, the canonical map $u: S \rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is surjective on edges), and two edges of $S$ represent the same morphism in $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{S}$ if and only if they are <em>homotopic</em> (Definition <a data-tag="003V" href="/tag/003V">1.4.3.1</a>). This leads to a more explicit description of the homotopy category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (generalizing Exercise <a data-tag="00HF" href="/tag/00HF">1.3.6.2</a>) which we will discuss in §<a data-tag="0049" href="/tag/0049">1.4.5</a> (see Proposition <a data-tag="004H" href="/tag/004H">1.4.5.7</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
